//// Decision 8 §4 — a value knows its own type. Two declarations with the same
//// fields are two types, so their values are never equal, however alike the
//// fields are.
////
//// This is the botopink-level statement of `13-module-identity`: the atom goes
//// inside the value. Nothing else in the suite asserts it. The cell needs no
//// union and no `unknown`, so what it fails on is identity and nothing else —
//// its siblings `type_identity_unknown`, `_union` and `_case` carry the rest.

type Person(name: string, age: i32)
type Vec(name: string, age: i32)

test "§4 two types with the same fields are different values" {
    val p = Person(name: "Ana", age: 30);
    val v = Vec(name: "Ana", age: 30);
    assert (p == v) == false;
}

//// Deliberately absent: `Person(name: "Ana") == Person(name: "Ana")`. Whether
//// two values of the *same* type with the same fields are equal is structural
//// equality, which no decision of this milestone legislates and no front owns —
//// it answers `false` on commonJS (reference equality on the class instance)
//// and `true` on erlang (one map). Reported to the maintainer rather than
//// listed against an invented row.
